Basic Formal Ontology (BFO) and Common Core Ontologies (CCO)

Basic Formal Ontology (BFO)

Basic Formal Ontology (BFO) is a top-level ontology that provides a small, well-defined set of categories for describing what exists in reality. It is designed to be domain-neutral and realist, meaning it aims to represent entities as they exist in the world rather than concepts, words, or database structures.

BFO distinguishes fundamental kinds of entities such as:
	•	Continuants (entities that persist through time, like objects and qualities)
	•	Occurrents (entities that unfold over time, like processes and events)
	•	Independent and dependent entities
	•	Universals and particulars

BFO does not attempt to model domain-specific knowledge. Instead, it provides a foundational framework that ensures consistency, logical coherence, and interoperability across ontologies built on top of it.

