Common Core Ontologies (CCO)

Common Core Ontologies (CCO) is a suite of interoperable, mid-level ontologies built on BFO. CCO extends BFO by providing reusable domain-neutral concepts that commonly appear across applied and enterprise domains, such as agents, organizations, artifacts, information content, roles, and events.

While BFO defines what kinds of things exist at the most general level, CCO provides practical modeling patterns and shared definitions that ontology engineers use to build consistent domain ontologies. This makes CCO especially valuable for enterprise ontology development, data integration, and knowledge graph architectures.

CCO is designed to:
	•	promote semantic interoperability across systems and datasets,
	•	support reusable design patterns,
	•	enforce clear Aristotelian definitions,
	•	and enable reasoning and validation through alignment with formal logic.

In ontology engineering workflows, CCO is commonly used alongside tools such as Protégé, ROBOT, SHACL, and SPARQL to support modeling, validation, and release engineering.

⸻

Relationship Between BFO and CCO (Summary)
	•	BFO provides the upper-level ontological framework
	•	CCO provides mid-level, reusable content and patterns
	•	Ontologies built with CCO inherit BFO’s logical structure and realism while gaining practical modeling components

